---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
/* R255 [§S2]: the headline years are derived from the phase map and gated
   against the ramps that deliver them; this page renders them, and does not
   restate any milestone year of its own. */
import {
  GATES, gateIsMeasurable, ROLLOUT_HEADLINES, ROLLOUT_YEARS
} from '../lib/rollout';
/* R292 [S12]: the timeline's label spelled its own span as a word. */
const rolloutYears = ROLLOUT_YEARS;
/* R256 [§S2]: the calendar anchor is stated in params.ts and referenced here. */
/* R253 + R156 [§S5]: the envelope is computed from transitionTotal and
   itCapital rather than typed, and the cost bridge's exclusion is named here,
   where the envelope is displayed. */
import { CALENDAR_ANCHOR_NOTE, transitionEnvelope } from '../lib/params';

/* R257 [§S11b]: five gates state a threshold and three state conditions. The
   split is counted from the floor text, not typed, so a gate that gains a
   number moves the sentence with it. */
const measurableGates = GATES.filter(gateIsMeasurable).length;
const qualitativeGates = GATES.length - measurableGates;

const ENV = transitionEnvelope();
const t = (b: number) => '$' + (b / 1000).toFixed(1) + 'T';
const ENVELOPE_RANGE = t(ENV.low) + '–' + t(ENV.high);
const ENVELOPE_MODE = t(ENV.mode);
const ENVELOPE_NOTE =
  'Derived from the model’s own transition-protection and information-mesh ' +
  'capital parameters (' + t(ENV.low) + '–' + t(ENV.high) + ', central ' +
  t(ENV.mode) + '), not typed. The framework’s controlled envelope is ' +
  t(ENV.frameworkLow) + '–' + t(ENV.frameworkHigh) + '; the model is wider on ' +
  'both ends because it prices the information mesh separately and holds a ' +
  'wider band on transition protection. This spending is cumulative, so it is ' +
  'not in the cost-bridge chart on the healthcare chapter, which decomposes a ' +
  'single mature year (2041) by which point transition outlays have wound ' +
  'down to zero.';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="National Health Assurance: Phased Rollout"
  description="What gets built in each year of the twelve-year rollout, and the evidence each gate requires before the next wave begins."
>
  <main>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Twelve years, sequenced by readiness</h2>
    <p class="desc">The plan defines a foundational Phase 0 followed by
      eight delivery phases. The dates are anchors, not automatic deadlines:
      independent readiness gates can hold, resize, or roll back a wave before
      the next major commitment. Select a milestone to see what is built and
      what evidence permits the program to continue.</p>
    <div class="tiles rollout-summary" aria-label="Rollout headline milestones">
      {ROLLOUT_HEADLINES.map((h) => (
        <div class="tile">
          <div class="label">{h.label}</div>
          <div class="value">{h.value}</div>
          <div class="range">{h.range}</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div id="rollout-timeline" class="rollout-timeline"
      aria-label={"Interactive " + rolloutYears + "-year phase timeline"}></div>
    <div id="rollout-detail" class="rollout-detail" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <p class="note">{CALENDAR_ANCHOR_NOTE}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>How the whole system moves together</h2>
    <p class="desc">Coverage cannot outrun identity and payment; cost-sharing
      cannot disappear before staffed diagnostic and specialist capacity
      exists; expanded benefits cannot outrun workforce and home-care
      readiness. The lanes below preserve all thirteen domain workstreams in
      the integrated roadmap.</p>
    <div class="tbl-scroll">
      <table class="data rollout-matrix" id="rollout-domain-matrix">
        <caption class="sr-only">Thirteen rollout domains across five phase bands</caption>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The two largest physical buildouts</h2>
    <p class="desc">The delivery network and public medicines capacity start
      before national coverage conversion and keep scaling after it. Numeric
      unit-network thresholds are readiness floors, while the manufacturing
      sequence is capability-based.</p>
    <div class="buildout-grid">
      <div class="buildout-panel">
        <h3>Community diagnostic-treatment network</h3>
        <div id="unit-ramp" class="unit-ramp"
          aria-label="Diagnostic-treatment unit network scale-up"></div>
        <p class="note">Type A micro, Type B neighborhood, Type C rural
          enhanced, and Type D urban/public-health units. Phase 5 reaches at
          least 65% population coverage; Gate 2 requires at least 80% before
          broad cost-sharing elimination; maturity requires at least 95%
          within the access standard and at least 15,000 certified units.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="buildout-panel">
        <h3>Medicines, diagnostics, and public production</h3>
        <div id="drug-pipeline" class="drug-pipeline"
          aria-label="Public drug purchasing and manufacturing rollout"></div>
        <p class="note">The pharmacy claims utility, national purchasing,
          public manufacturing, formulary governance, shortage controls,
          supplier qualification, inventory visibility, and backup supply
          scale as one connected capability, not as a stand-alone factory.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Who carries the rollout</h2>
    <p class="desc">The changeover deliberately separates transition command,
      daily operations and payment, and independent rights and verification.
      That prevents the organization being evaluated from grading its own
      readiness.</p>
    <div id="rollout-agencies" class="agency-grid"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>{GATES.length} gates that can stop the clock</h2>
    <p class="desc">A gate is a decision, not a date. If evidence is below the
      floor, the plan preserves care and payment, invokes a manual or
      legacy fallback, repairs the defect, and re-enters review.</p>
    <p class="desc">{measurableGates} of the {GATES.length} set a threshold a
      number can be tested against. The other {qualitativeGates} set conditions
      instead: a list of capabilities that must be working before the wave
      proceeds, judged by the named body rather than scored. They are real
      requirements and they are not measurements, and each says so on its own
      card, because a page that presents all {GATES.length} identically invites
      a reader to assume all {GATES.length} are the same kind of claim.</p>
    <div id="rollout-gates" class="gate-grid"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Protected transition work</h2>
    <p class="desc">Incremental transition and implementation cost runs over
      10–12 years and is not straight-lined: the model carries an annual
      outlay profile, and the envelope below is the total of it. Every dollar
      must attach to a costed work package, deliverable, owner, gate,
      contingency, and transfer into mature operations.</p>
    <div class="transition-envelope" aria-label="Transition cost envelope">
      <div class="transition-envelope-value">{ENVELOPE_RANGE}</div>
      <div>
        <b>Cumulative transition envelope, central estimate {ENVELOPE_MODE}</b>
        <span>continuity, infrastructure, capacity, migration, stabilization,
          workforce, and wind-down, plus the information-mesh build-out
          capital, and not mature annual operating cost</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">{ENVELOPE_NOTE}</p>
    <div id="rollout-workstreams" class="workstream-grid"></div>
    <p class="note" id="rollout-workstream-note"></p>
    <p class="note">A gate hold cannot cut mandatory care, medication,
      provider-payment, appeal, or continuity fallbacks. It changes the pace
      or scope of new deployment and triggers repair.</p>
  </section>

  <p class="rollout-source">
    <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and methodology</a>.</p>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/rollout-client.ts';
</script>
